# 029 · Full API and MCP coverage

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.2.0](../roadmap.md#v020)

## What

Every dashboard action gets an API call and an MCP tool: Providers (add, check and delete, never echoing a secret), Add a rig and its lifecycle, Edit rig, New campaign, Try the kit, another run, reviews and endings. Owner-only actions stay owner-only. An OpenAPI file describes control and the harness. A test checks that every route is registered and that every dashboard action has a twin.

## Why

Other agents must be able to run Thermite through control without the dashboard.

## Done when

- [ ] The twin test passes.
- [ ] An agent runs a campaign from start to end over MCP only.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-19, owner: Control needs full API and MCP coverage of its features, so other agents can work with it.
- 2026-10-20, builder, within the v0.1.0 build brief (Thermite runs itself, so an operator agent must start a run without the browser): **Start a run** gets its twin first. The twin is `POST /v1/campaigns/<campaign>/runs`, MCP's `start_run` and `thermite run start`. All three call the dashboard's own code for the panel (`ControlActions.startRun`, filled in by `dashboard.ts`; the shared request and answer are in `src/control/start-run.ts`), so they check and refuse in the panel's words.
  - **Fields:** the ones the panel offers: the rig, agents on or off, the agents' model, promotion, GPUs for the judge, a run of the campaign to copy untried experiments from, and past research by knowledge-base id. The panel offers no lanes and no harness, so the twins don't either.
  - **Tokens:** REST and the command line take the owner's token, as every REST route does. MCP takes the owner's and an operator's: an operator may already start runs with `issue_command`'s `assign_campaign`, and may give a rig's agents a model with `set_rig_model`. An operator's `start_run` can't set the GPU split, because `issue_command` sends no `rig_settings`. It can't start a stopped RunPod pod either, as for `assign_campaign`. The agent's token may issue `assign_campaign` too, but it has no `start_run`: the panel is the owner's, and it reads the knowledge base, which the agent's token can't.
  - **Differences from the panel:** for a try (agents off), the twins refuse a model, a run to copy from and past research, where the panel leaves them out. The run to copy from must be a run of the campaign, as the panel lists them; the panel's form now refuses another one too.
- 2026-10-21, owner: "yeah add the MCP/REST endpoints": each agent's model on **Edit rig** gets its twin, so a goal campaign's advisors can use a ChatGPT subscription's model while the workers and the planner use another.
  - **Builder, 2026-10-21:** `set_rig_model` and `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/model` take `agent` (`workers`, the default, `planner`, `teachers`, `combiner`, `auditor` or `panel`, as Edit rig and control's records name them) and `clear: true`, which sends the agent back to its default as the first choice in its row does. `clear` reads better than an empty model, and it is refused with any other field but `agent`. Both call Edit rig's own code for the row (`ControlActions.setRigModel`; the shared fields and answer are in `src/control/rig-model.ts`), so they check, refuse and audit alike. No signed command carries these models: the rig reads them from control with its model route (the planner) and its advisor routes, as after Edit rig.
  - **Builder, 2026-10-21:** `harness` and `context_window` stay the workers' alone, and are refused in words with another agent: Edit rig has no harness choice and no cap for the other rows.
  - **2026-10-21, goal 073:** `harness` is now taken for every agent, after the owner's "Full choice for every agent"; [goal 073](_completed/073-harness-for-each-agent.goal) has how.
  - **Builder, 2026-10-21:** each agent's model is read with MCP's `rig_models` (the owner's, an operator's and a reader's token, as `providers`) and `GET /v1/rigs/<rig>/model` (the owner's), and the answer of a change lists them too.
- 2026-10-22, builder, within the brief for the rest of **Edit rig** (its parts, workers at a time, limits, promotion rule, exports and GPU split): the twin is `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/settings` and MCP's `set_rig_settings`, read with `GET /v1/rigs/<rig>/settings` and MCP's `rig_settings`. Edit rig's check moved out of the dashboard into `src/control/rig-settings.ts` (`rigSettingsChange`), which **Save changes** and both twins call. So all three send only what differs from what the rig reports, check the judge's share against the rig's GPU split, parse with the `rig_settings` payload, and refuse in the page's words (`Not saved: …`). The page's behaviour and text are unchanged. Unlike the models' twins, no `ControlActions` callback: the check needs only control's store and signing key, so REST and MCP call the module directly, as for a campaign's context settings.
  - **Fields:** the `rig_settings` payload's, each optional, with its types in the request and its ranges refused in its words. `judge_gpus: null` (the rig's default split), and `trial_minutes: null` and `max_turns: null` (no limit; goal 079 for a trial's length), are taken as the payload takes them. A field equal to what the rig reports is no change, so a caller may send back what it read. Two refusals the page never needs, as it has no field for them: a rig whose harness is older and reports no settings, and a number for `judge_gpus` on a rig with no GPU set (in Start a run's words).
  - **Tokens:** REST takes the owner's token, as every route. MCP's `set_rig_settings` takes the owner's and an operator's; an operator's call that would change `promotion` or `judge_gpus` is refused in words (`Only the owner's token changes a rig's promotion rule: leave out promotion.`, and the GPU split's words of `start_run`). Reason: `rig_settings` is an owner-only kind in the harness, but the operator acts for the owner, and its `owner:operator` passes that gate, as for the queue's kinds and `message_agent`. The parts, workers at a time and limits steer a rig, as the operator already does. The promotion rule decides whether a confirmed change waits for the owner, whose approval the operator never gives (`approve_promotion` is the owner's alone). A new GPU split restarts the rig's harness, which `start_run` already keeps from an operator. The agent's token has neither tool: it has no `set_rig_model` either, and a rig's settings are the people's who run it, not a campaign's agents'. `rig_settings` is read with the owner's, an operator's and a reader's token, as `rig_models`.
  - **Command line:** no twin, as the command line has none for the agents' models to mirror.

## Open questions

None.

## Progress

- 2026-10-19: Goal written from the roadmap.
- 2026-10-20: **Start a run**'s twin is built: `POST /v1/campaigns/<campaign>/runs`, MCP `start_run` and `thermite run start`, with the panel's refusals in words and a stopped RunPod pod started first (the owner's token only). Tests: `tests/control/start-run.test.ts` (REST against the panel's own assignment, every refusal, a model given first, MCP tokens), `tests/control/runpod-lifecycle.test.ts` (the pod started first; an operator refused), `tests/cli/run.test.ts`. Pages: the REST, MCP and command-line references, **Start a run later** in Start a campaign from the dashboard, and the pages that listed the ways to start or copy a run. The rest of the goal is not started: the other dashboard actions, the OpenAPI file and the twin test.
- 2026-10-21: Each agent's model on **Edit rig** has its twin: `set_rig_model` and `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/model` take `agent` and `clear`, and `rig_models` and `GET /v1/rigs/<rig>/model` read each agent's model. An advisor can take a ChatGPT subscription's model; it already could on Edit rig, and the supervisor runs it with read-only tools on the subscription's access token. Tests: `tests/control/rig-model.test.ts` (each agent set and cleared over REST and MCP, the routes the rig then reads, every refusal, the tokens), with `context-cap`, `mcp` and `dashboard` tests still passing. Pages: the MCP and REST references, Tokens, Give each agent its own model (**Over MCP or REST**), Script Thermite with the REST API and Let an agent run Thermite over MCP. The rest of Edit rig (its parts, limits, promotion rule and GPU split) still has no twin.
- 2026-10-22: The rest of **Edit rig** has its twin: `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/settings` and MCP's `set_rig_settings` change a rig's parts, workers at a time, a trial's length and turns, its promotion rule and its GPU split through Save changes' own check (`src/control/rig-settings.ts`), sending only what differs as one signed `rig_settings`; `GET /v1/rigs/<rig>/settings` and `rig_settings` read what the rig reports, with its GPU split. An operator changes all but the promotion rule and the GPU split. Tests: `tests/control/rig-settings.test.ts` (each field over REST and MCP, nothing changed, every refusal, the tokens, and the rig's harness taking an operator's change and reporting it), with the `dashboard`, `edit-rig-agents`, `mcp`, `rig-model`, `start-run` and `context-cap` tests still passing. Pages: the MCP and REST references, Tokens, Signed commands, Limits and pacing, Script Thermite with the REST API (**Change a rig's settings**), Let an agent run Thermite over MCP, Give a rig several GPUs, Decide what waits for you, and Sharing a device. Every part of Edit rig now has a twin. The rest of the goal is not started: the other dashboard actions, the OpenAPI file and the twin test.

